<h1>Clustering with Raft</h1>
<p class="lede">
  Run three brokers as one cluster and every partition is replicated: a write is acknowledged once a majority of the
  brokers hold it, and the cluster keeps serving if one broker fails.
</p>

<h2>How it fits together</h2>
<p>
  Each partition is its own Raft group, with its own leader. All groups on a broker share one listener and one
  connection to each other broker; every frame names the group it belongs to.
</p>
<dl class="defs">
  <div><dt>Node</dt><dd>Elects a leader, replicates the log, decides what is committed.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Transport</dt><dd>One authenticated connection to each peer, carrying every partition's messages.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Apply task</dt><dd>Writes committed entries into the partition's segment files and answers the waiting producer.</dd></div>
</dl>

<h2>Start a three-node cluster</h2>
<p>
  Start each broker with its own id, the address its peers reach it on, every other member, and the same secret.
  The membership is exactly the brokers you list; there is no discovery. To change it later, see
  <a href="#adding-and-removing-brokers">Adding and removing brokers</a>.
</p>
<figure class="code">
  <figcaption><span>node 1</span></figcaption>
<pre><span class="c-key">export</span> ES_RAFT_SHARED_SECRET=<span class="c-str">'output of openssl rand -hex 32, the same on every node'</span>
es-broker --data-dir /var/lib/es --bind 10.0.0.1:9000 --auth required \
  --raft-node-id 1 --raft-bind 10.0.0.1:6000 \
  --raft-peer 2=10.0.0.2:6000 \
  --raft-peer 3=10.0.0.3:6000</pre>
</figure>
<figure class="code">
  <figcaption><span>node 2</span></figcaption>
<pre>es-broker --data-dir /var/lib/es --bind 10.0.0.2:9000 --auth required \
  --raft-node-id 2 --raft-bind 10.0.0.2:6000 \
  --raft-peer 1=10.0.0.1:6000 \
  --raft-peer 3=10.0.0.3:6000</pre>
</figure>
<figure class="code">
  <figcaption><span>node 3</span></figcaption>
<pre>es-broker --data-dir /var/lib/es --bind 10.0.0.3:9000 --auth required \
  --raft-node-id 3 --raft-bind 10.0.0.3:6000 \
  --raft-peer 1=10.0.0.1:6000 \
  --raft-peer 2=10.0.0.2:6000</pre>
</figure>
<p>
  Then create topics on every broker with the same name and partition count. Each partition elects a leader within a
  few hundred milliseconds.
</p>
<aside class="note">
  <p>Use an odd number of brokers</p>
  <p>Three tolerate one failure, five tolerate two. Four tolerate only one, like three, and cost more.</p>
</aside>
<p>
  The broker refuses a configuration that cannot work: a node id of 0, a peer listed twice or sharing an address, a node
  listing itself, or a Raft port reachable from the network without a shared secret or mTLS.
</p>

<h3>A single node</h3>
<p>
  A broker with <code>--raft-node-id</code> and no peers is a cluster of one. It elects itself and commits immediately,
  which is useful for trying the replicated write path locally:
</p>
<figure class="code">
  <figcaption><span>shell</span></figcaption>
<pre>es-broker --data-dir ./data --raft-node-id 1 --raft-bind 127.0.0.1:6000</pre>
</figure>

<h2>Writing and reading</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Produce to the leader.</strong> A produce sent to a follower fails with a hint naming the leader; send it there instead. <code>GET /raft</code> shows each partition's leader.</li>
  <li><strong>Acknowledgement means a majority has it.</strong> The leader appends the entry durably, replicates it, and answers once a majority of members have it on disk and it is applied.</li>
  <li><strong>Reads come from what a broker has applied.</strong> A follower can be slightly behind the leader; reads are not linearizable.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What happens to a write</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><p><strong>Propose.</strong> The leader turns the request into one log entry holding all its records, the offset of the first, and their timestamps. Every replica will store the same records at the same offsets.</p></li>
  <li><p><strong>Replicate.</strong> The leader appends the entry to its log and sends it to each follower, in batches of at most 1,024 entries or 4&nbsp;MiB. A follower checks the entry follows on from what it has, appends it durably, and confirms.</p></li>
  <li><p><strong>Commit.</strong> Once a majority holds an entry from the leader's current term, it is committed, along with everything before it.</p></li>
  <li><p><strong>Apply.</strong> Each broker writes committed records into its partition files under the leader's offsets. The producer that proposed the entry gets its offsets; it is matched by a proposal id, so it is never handed another leader's entry by mistake.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="small">
  A new leader appends an empty entry first, so entries left uncommitted by its predecessor are committed without
  waiting for the next client write.
</p>

<h2>Snapshots and catching up</h2>
<p>
  The partition files already hold the data, so the Raft log does not need to keep it forever. Every
  <code>snapshot_after_applies</code> entries (1,024 by default) a broker fsyncs the partition and drops the log entries
  it has applied.
</p>
<p>
  A broker that was down long enough, or a new one, may need entries the leader has already dropped. The leader then
  streams it the records it is missing straight from its own partition, in 1&nbsp;MiB chunks, each acknowledged with
  the follower's new position. When the follower has everything up to the snapshot, ordinary replication resumes.
</p>

<h2>Transport and security</h2>
<p>
  Brokers authenticate each other with the shared secret, without sending it: each side proves it knows the secret
  against a random challenge from the other, so a recorded handshake cannot be replayed. The dialing broker proves
  itself first, and the listening broker answers with its own proof only once the dialer's has checked out, so a
  connection that has proved nothing learns nothing derived from the secret. The dialing broker checks it reached the
  node it meant to; the listening broker checks the dialer is a current member, and closes connections from any address
  that is not a peer's before they reach the handshake.
</p>
<p>
  The secret must be at least 32 bytes; the broker refuses to start with a shorter one. Generate it with
  <code>openssl rand -hex 32</code> and pass it in <code>ES_RAFT_SHARED_SECRET</code> or in a file named by
  <code>--raft-shared-secret-file</code>, readable by its owner only. On the command line it would be visible to every
  local account in the process list.
</p>
<p>
  After the handshake every frame carries a MAC over a sequence number, so frames cannot be forged, replayed or
  reordered by anyone without the secret. Messages from outside the membership, or with an implausibly large term, are
  ignored.
</p>
<p>
  A connection with nothing to carry sends a keepalive every 5 seconds, in both directions. A broker that hears nothing
  on a connection for 15 seconds closes it, and the dialing side redials. A peer that vanished without closing its
  connections, after a power cut or a dropped route, is noticed within that time instead of hours later.
</p>

<h3>Per-broker identity with mTLS</h3>
<p>
  There is one secret for the whole cluster, so on its own it separates members from outsiders, not members from each
  other: a broker with the secret can connect as any member and vote, acknowledge or replicate in its name. To tell
  members apart, give every broker its own certificate, signed by a CA the members share:
</p>
<figure class="code">
  <figcaption><span>node 1</span></figcaption>
<pre>es-broker --data-dir /var/lib/es --bind 10.0.0.1:9000 --auth required \
  --raft-node-id 1 --raft-bind 10.0.0.1:6000 \
  --raft-peer 2=10.0.0.2:6000 --raft-peer 3=10.0.0.3:6000 \
  --raft-tls-cert /etc/es/raft/node1.pem \
  --raft-tls-key /etc/es/raft/node1.key \
  --raft-tls-ca /etc/es/raft/ca.pem</pre>
</figure>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The certificate names the broker.</strong> Node 7's certificate carries the subjectAltName
    <code>DNS:es-node-7</code>, and its extended key usage allows both <code>serverAuth</code> and
    <code>clientAuth</code>, because every broker both listens and dials. A broker refuses to start with a certificate
    that does not name its own <code>--raft-node-id</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>The listener takes the dialer's id from its certificate.</strong> A dialer must present a certificate the
    CA signed, naming exactly one current member; if the id it announces in the handshake is not that one, the
    connection is refused. Holding node 2's certificate does not let a broker speak as node 3.</li>
  <li><strong>The dialer checks the listener's certificate</strong> names the node it meant to reach, and refuses a
    certificate for any other node.</li>
  <li><strong>Traffic is encrypted.</strong> Every connection is TLS 1.3.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  With mTLS the shared secret is optional, even on a routable address; set both and the secret's handshake runs inside
  TLS. Enable mTLS on every member at the same restart: a broker with it and a broker without it cannot connect.
</p>
<figure class="code">
  <figcaption><span>a certificate for node 1, with openssl</span></figcaption>
<pre>openssl req -new -newkey ec -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-256 -nodes \
  -keyout node1.key -subj <span class="c-str">'/CN=es-node-1'</span> -out node1.csr
printf <span class="c-str">'subjectAltName=DNS:es-node-1\nextendedKeyUsage=serverAuth,clientAuth\n'</span> &gt; node1.ext
openssl x509 -req -in node1.csr -CA ca.pem -CAkey ca.key -days 365 -extfile node1.ext -out node1.pem</pre>
</figure>
<aside class="note warn">
  <p>Without mTLS, replication traffic is not encrypted</p>
  <p>It is authenticated and tamper-proof, but readable on the wire. Keep <code>--raft-bind</code> on a private network,
    or configure <code>--raft-tls-*</code>.</p>
</aside>

<h3>Upgrading from 0.2</h3>
<p>
  0.3 brokers speak version 3 of the handshake (<code>RAF3</code>), in which the dialer proves itself first. A 0.2
  broker speaks version 2 (<code>RAF2</code>), in which the listener did, and a 0.3 broker refuses it by default. To
  upgrade one broker at a time, start each upgraded broker with <code>--raft-legacy-handshake</code>: it then accepts
  version 2 from the brokers still on 0.2, and when a 0.2 broker closes its version-3 hello unanswered, it retries in
  version 2. Once every member runs 0.3, restart each without the flag. The flag is deprecated and goes away in 0.4; it
  cannot be combined with mTLS, which 0.2 does not speak. Do not change the membership during the upgrade.
</p>
<p class="small">
  Without a secret or mTLS the same handshake runs with no authentication, and the broker only allows that on a
  loopback address. Brokers from before 0.2 (handshake <code>RAFT</code>) cannot join; upgrade them to 0.2 first. Raft
  state from older versions is converted on first start.
</p>

<h2>Durability of Raft state</h2>
<ul>
  <li>The term and vote are in a small file replaced atomically whenever they change.</li>
  <li>The log is append-only: one write and one fsync per batch of entries.</li>
  <li>Nothing that depends on a change is sent before the change is on disk.</li>
  <li>If writing fails, the node stops instead of answering, so it can never vote twice in a term or confirm entries it does not have.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="adding-and-removing-brokers">Adding and removing brokers</h2>
<p>
  The membership can change while the cluster runs, one broker at a time, through
  <a href="<%= docs_api_path() %>">/admin/raft/members</a>. Each change is a single-server step: it adds or removes one
  voter, takes effect in a partition as soon as it is in that partition's log, and the next change waits until it has
  committed. Any majority before the step and any majority after it share a broker, so the cluster can never elect two
  leaders while it changes. The transport follows: an added broker is dialed and accepted, a removed one is
  disconnected and refused, with no restart.
</p>
<p>
  Each partition has its own leader, and only that leader can change it. A request changes the partitions the
  broker it is sent to leads, and lists the others with their leader. So send each change to every broker, and check
  that every partition reports <code>committed</code> or <code>already</code>.
</p>

<h3>Add a broker</h3>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><p><strong>Start it as a joiner.</strong> Give it its id, its Raft address, the current members with
    <code>--raft-peer</code>, the cluster's secret or a certificate naming it, and <code>--raft-join</code>. It replicates
    whatever it is sent but never campaigns until it is a member.</p></li>
  <li><p><strong>Create every topic on it</strong>, with the same names and partition counts.</p></li>
  <li><p><strong>Add it on every broker.</strong> Each adds it to the partitions it leads; the leader then streams it
    the records it is missing.</p></li>
  <li><p><strong>Check.</strong> <code>GET /admin/raft/members</code> on the new broker lists it among the voters of
    every partition. Add <code>--raft-peer 4=…</code> to the other brokers' configuration for their next restart.</p></li>
</ol>
<figure class="code">
  <figcaption><span>node 4, then each member</span></figcaption>
<pre>es-broker --data-dir /var/lib/es --bind 10.0.0.4:9000 --auth required \
  --raft-node-id 4 --raft-bind 10.0.0.4:6000 --raft-join \
  --raft-peer 1=10.0.0.1:6000 --raft-peer 2=10.0.0.2:6000 --raft-peer 3=10.0.0.3:6000

<span class="c-key">for</span> b <span class="c-key">in</span> 10.0.0.1 10.0.0.2 10.0.0.3; <span class="c-key">do</span>
  curl -s -X POST -H <span class="c-str">"authorization: Bearer $ADMIN_KEY"</span> \
    -H <span class="c-str">"content-type: application/json"</span> $b:9000/admin/raft/members \
    -d <span class="c-str">'{"id": 4, "addr": "10.0.0.4:6000"}'</span>
<span class="c-key">done</span></pre>
</figure>

<h3>Remove a broker</h3>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><p><strong>Remove it on every broker, itself included.</strong> <code>DELETE /admin/raft/members/3</code>. Where it
    leads, it manages its own removal, without counting itself, and steps down once the removal commits; the others then
    elect a leader among themselves.</p></li>
  <li><p><strong>Stop it</strong> once every partition reports the removal. The others have already disconnected it and
    refuse its connections.</p></li>
  <li><p><strong>Drop its <code>--raft-peer</code></strong> from the others' configuration.</p></li>
</ol>
<aside class="note">
  <p>One change at a time</p>
  <p>A request is refused with 409 while any partition on that broker has a change that has not committed. Finish one
    change on every broker before starting the next, and do not create topics in between: a new topic starts from the
    membership its broker sees at that moment. To replace a broker, add the new one first, then remove the old one.</p>
</aside>
<p class="small">
  A restarted broker takes its membership from each partition's log and snapshot, not from <code>--raft-peer</code>;
  the flags matter for topics with no recorded change, and for brokers started fresh.
</p>

<h2>Checking a cluster</h2>
<figure class="code">
  <figcaption><span>shell</span></figcaption>
<pre>curl -s -H <span class="c-str">"authorization: Bearer $ADMIN_KEY"</span> 10.0.0.1:9000/raft</pre>
</figure>
<p>
  <code>/raft</code> lists every replicated partition with its role, term, leader and commit index, and whether this
  broker is replicating: as leader, a majority has caught up; as follower, it knows the leader. Ask every member; the
  leader's answer proves a majority exists, and each follower's proves it is part of it.
</p>

<h2>Limitations</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>No leader forwarding.</strong> Clients must send produces to the leader.</li>
  <li><strong>Membership changes go to each leader.</strong> A change is applied by the leader of each partition, so it is sent to every broker. One broker at a time; there is no joint consensus.</li>
  <li><strong>No linearizable reads.</strong> A follower serves what it has applied.</li>
  <li><strong>No pre-vote.</strong> A rejoining broker cannot win an election against a healthy leader, but its higher term can make the leader step down once.</li>
  <li><strong>Topics are created per broker.</strong> Create each topic, with the same partition count, on every member.</li>
</ul>
